=head2 SDL_RenderTexture9Grid

  my $result = SDL_RenderTexture9Grid($renderer, $texture, $srcrect, $left_width, $right_width, $top_height, $bottom_height, $scale, $dstrect);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_RenderTexture9Grid(SDL_Renderer * renderer, SDL_Texture * texture, const SDL_FRect * srcrect, float left_width, float right_width, float top_height, float bottom_height, float scale, const SDL_FRect * dstrect) >>

Perform a scaled copy using the 9-grid algorithm to the current rendering target at subpixel precision.

The pixels in the texture are split into a 3x3 grid, using the different corner sizes for each corner, and the sides and center making up the remaining pixels. The corners are then scaled using `scale` and fit into the corners of the destination rectangle. The sides and center are then stretched into place to cover the remaining destination rectangle.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

=over

=item renderer - SDL3::Renderer object or undef. the renderer which should copy parts of a texture.

=item texture - SDL3::Texture object or undef. the source texture.

=item srcrect - SDL3::FRect object or undef. the SDL_Rect structure representing the rectangle to be used for the 9-grid, or NULL to use the entire texture.

=item left_width - number. the width, in pixels, of the left corners in `srcrect`.

=item right_width - number. the width, in pixels, of the right corners in `srcrect`.

=item top_height - number. the height, in pixels, of the top corners in `srcrect`.

=item bottom_height - number. the height, in pixels, of the bottom corners in `srcrect`.

=item scale - number. the scale used to transform the corner of `srcrect` into the corner of `dstrect`, or 0.0f for an unscaled copy.

=item dstrect - SDL3::FRect object or undef. a pointer to the destination rectangle, or NULL for the entire rendering target.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_RenderTexture9GridTiled

  my $result = SDL_RenderTexture9GridTiled($renderer, $texture, $srcrect, $left_width, $right_width, $top_height, $bottom_height, $scale, $dstrect, $tileScale);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_RenderTexture9GridTiled(SDL_Renderer * renderer, SDL_Texture * texture, const SDL_FRect * srcrect, float left_width, float right_width, float top_height, float bottom_height, float scale, const SDL_FRect * dstrect, float tileScale) >>

Perform a scaled copy using the 9-grid algorithm to the current rendering target at subpixel precision.

The pixels in the texture are split into a 3x3 grid, using the different corner sizes for each corner, and the sides and center making up the remaining pixels. The corners are then scaled using `scale` and fit into the corners of the destination rectangle. The sides and center are then tiled into place to cover the remaining destination rectangle.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

=over

=item renderer - SDL3::Renderer object or undef. the renderer which should copy parts of a texture.

=item texture - SDL3::Texture object or undef. the source texture.

=item srcrect - SDL3::FRect object or undef. the SDL_Rect structure representing the rectangle to be used for the 9-grid, or NULL to use the entire texture.

=item left_width - number. the width, in pixels, of the left corners in `srcrect`.

=item right_width - number. the width, in pixels, of the right corners in `srcrect`.

=item top_height - number. the height, in pixels, of the top corners in `srcrect`.

=item bottom_height - number. the height, in pixels, of the bottom corners in `srcrect`.

=item scale - number. the scale used to transform the corner of `srcrect` into the corner of `dstrect`, or 0.0f for an unscaled copy.

=item dstrect - SDL3::FRect object or undef. a pointer to the destination rectangle, or NULL for the entire rendering target.

=item tileScale - number. the scale used to transform the borders and center of `srcrect` into the borders and middle of `dstrect`, or 1.0f for an unscaled copy.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.4.0.

