Since: This function is available since SDL 3.4.0.

=head2 SDL_CreateGPURenderState

  my $result = SDL_CreateGPURenderState($renderer, $createinfo);

C: C<< SDL_GPURenderState * SDL_CreateGPURenderState(SDL_Renderer * renderer, const SDL_GPURenderStateCreateInfo * createinfo) >>

Create custom GPU render state.

This function should be called on the thread that created the renderer.

=over

=item renderer - SDL3::Renderer object or undef. the renderer to use.

=item createinfo - SDL3::GPURenderStateCreateInfo object or undef. a struct describing the GPU render state to create.

=back

Returns SDL3::GPURenderState object or undef: a custom GPU render state or NULL on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.4.0.

=head2 SDL_SetGPURenderStateFragmentUniforms

  my $result = SDL_SetGPURenderStateFragmentUniforms($state, $slot_index, $data, $length);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_SetGPURenderStateFragmentUniforms(SDL_GPURenderState * state, Uint32 slot_index, const void * data, Uint32 length) >>

Set fragment shader uniform variables in a custom GPU render state.

The data is copied and will be pushed using SDL_PushGPUFragmentUniformData() during draw call execution.

This function should be called on the thread that created the renderer.

=over

=item state - SDL3::GPURenderState object or undef. the state to modify.

=item slot_index - unsigned integer. the fragment uniform slot to push data to.

=item data - SDL3::Pointer, object, string buffer or undef. client data to write.

=item length - unsigned integer. the length of the data to write.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.4.0.

=head2 SDL_SetGPURenderState

  my $result = SDL_SetGPURenderState($renderer, $state);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_SetGPURenderState(SDL_Renderer * renderer, SDL_GPURenderState * state) >>

Set custom GPU render state.

This function sets custom GPU render state for subsequent draw calls. This allows using custom shaders with the GPU renderer.

This function should be called on the thread that created the renderer.

=over

=item renderer - SDL3::Renderer object or undef. the renderer to use.

=item state - SDL3::GPURenderState object or undef. the state to to use, or NULL to clear custom GPU render state.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.4.0.

=head2 SDL_DestroyGPURenderState

  SDL_DestroyGPURenderState($state);

C: C<< void SDL_DestroyGPURenderState(SDL_GPURenderState * state) >>

Destroy custom GPU render state.

This function should be called on the thread that created the renderer.

=over

=item state - SDL3::GPURenderState object or undef. the state to destroy.

=back

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.4.0.


=head1 ENUMERATIONS

Every enumerator is exported as a constant sub.

=head2 SDL_TextureAccess

The access pattern allowed for a texture.

=over

=item SDL_TEXTUREACCESS_STATIC = 0 - Changes rarely, not lockable

=item SDL_TEXTUREACCESS_STREAMING = 1 - Changes frequently, lockable

=item SDL_TEXTUREACCESS_TARGET = 2 - Texture can be used as a render target

=back

=head2 SDL_TextureAddressMode

The addressing mode for a texture when used in SDL_RenderGeometry().

=over

=item SDL_TEXTURE_ADDRESS_INVALID = -1

=item SDL_TEXTURE_ADDRESS_AUTO = 0 - Wrapping is enabled if texture coordinates are outside [0, 1], this is the default

=item SDL_TEXTURE_ADDRESS_CLAMP = 1 - Texture coordinates are clamped to the [0, 1] range

=item SDL_TEXTURE_ADDRESS_WRAP = 2 - The texture is repeated (tiled)

=back

=head2 SDL_RendererLogicalPresentation

How the logical size is mapped to the output.

=over

=item SDL_LOGICAL_PRESENTATION_DISABLED = 0 - There is no logical size in effect

=item SDL_LOGICAL_PRESENTATION_STRETCH = 1 - The rendered content is stretched to the output resolution

=item SDL_LOGICAL_PRESENTATION_LETTERBOX = 2 - The rendered content is fit to the largest dimension and the other dimension is letterboxed with the clear color

=item SDL_LOGICAL_PRESENTATION_OVERSCAN = 3 - The rendered content is fit to the smallest dimension and the other dimension extends beyond the output bounds

=item SDL_LOGICAL_PRESENTATION_INTEGER_SCALE = 4 - The rendered content is scaled up by integer multiples to fit the output resolution

=back



